 on a math exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $25$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although drawing a red card can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of books published cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b34d899f-07f1-5450-a677-a95d2bf81727"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b34d899f-07f1-5450-a677-a95d2bf81727" :answer-field/id #uuid "b34d899f-07f1-5450-a677-a95d2bf81727" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9cd34f00-e35e-5bc2-9e2e-d848768a619a" "answer-55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" "answer-d81e7ec7-8b44-5e18-b5c4-bed1de80a167" "answer-f3648a58-4da3-5f65-8f21-ee1b6cdfadce" "answer-c72a5b0e-4bd0-5a33-aae9-d61e9b22e3b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cd34f00-e35e-5bc2-9e2e-d848768a619a" :answer/id #uuid "9cd34f00-e35e-5bc2-9e2e-d848768a619a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/id #uuid "55192222-654e-569c-b331-381456217ef3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d81e7ec7-8b44-5e18-b5c4-bed1de80a167" :answer/id #uuid "d81e7ec7-8b44-5e18-b5c4-bed1de80a167" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3648a58-4da3-5f65-8f21-ee1b6cdfadce" :answer/id #uuid "f3648a58-4da3-5f65-8f21-ee1b6cdfadce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c72a5b0e-4bd0-5a33-aae9-d61e9b22e3b5" :answer/id #uuid "c72a5b0e-4bd0-5a33-aae9-d61e9b22e3b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e06f09da-f1cd-5f64-925a-f4c6e513e373" :question/id #uuid "e06f09da-f1cd-5f64-925a-f4c6e513e373" :question/math-academy-id "q-130361" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n\n- The number of trees in a forest of $300$ trees that have bird's nests\n- The number of vaccines administered in a country during one season\n- The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times" :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of trees that have bird's nests among $300$ trees can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $300$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although receiving a vaccine can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of odd numbers obtained when rolling an eight-sided die $10$ times can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389" :answer-field/id #uuid "dcab9192-f4e1-5668-9a13-95de83c01389" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-291531dd-6f7e-59b2-9b00-3a0fc4cc8ff1" "answer-4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" "answer-970d7ed7-c547-5138-9f77-c9dd3621443a" "answer-d0c5e69a-ff05-5da9-a145-db810e12de50" "answer-1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-291531dd-6f7e-59b2-9b00-3a0fc4cc8ff1" :answer/id #uuid "291531dd-6f7e-59b2-9b00-3a0fc4cc8ff1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/id #uuid "4e50fb71-af5c-5be3-a593-2630c7e49e54" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-970d7ed7-c547-5138-9f77-c9dd3621443a" :answer/id #uuid "970d7ed7-c547-5138-9f77-c9dd3621443a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0c5e69a-ff05-5da9-a145-db810e12de50" :answer/id #uuid "d0c5e69a-ff05-5da9-a145-db810e12de50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101" :answer/id #uuid "1c395945-c14b-5e42-8504-6dd097d78101" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8e0e75d6-02e9-556c-b809-4824d53487ed" :question/id #uuid "8e0e75d6-02e9-556c-b809-4824d53487ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-89380" :question/problem "A fair $5$-sided die with numbers $1$ through $5$ is rolled $9$ times. What is the probability of getting a $1$ or a $2$ exactly five times? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $1$ or a $2$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=9$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a $1$ or $2$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(9,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=5$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{9}{5}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{9-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9!}{5!(9-5)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =126{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{5}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{4} \\\\  & =0.167\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6e2ae69-22b8-5724-b660-3eef8661b061"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6e2ae69-22b8-5724-b660-3eef8661b061" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6e2ae69-22b8-5724-b660-3eef8661b061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eff5311f-f548-5e70-829c-1a093a0352eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fce3391-052e-587a-a9de-fd2fea484deb" "answer-3301780f-7d13-59bf-babc-3915b8c2d051" "answer-eff5311f-f548-5e70-829c-1a093a0352eb" "answer-90225c0b-d22b-5ec7-82e0-736f61864ff1" "answer-e928e1f5-d998-5632-b572-4eff4fc046da"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fce3391-052e-587a-a9de-fd2fea484deb" :answer/id #uuid "2fce3391-052e-587a-a9de-fd2fea484deb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.097" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3301780f-7d13-59bf-babc-3915b8c2d051" :answer/id #uuid "3301780f-7d13-59bf-babc-3915b8c2d051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.125" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eff5311f-f548-5e70-829c-1a093a0352eb" :answer/id #uuid "eff5311f-f548-5e70-829c-1a093a0352eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.167" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90225c0b-d22b-5ec7-82e0-736f61864ff1" :answer/id #uuid "90225c0b-d22b-5ec7-82e0-736f61864ff1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.184" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e928e1f5-d998-5632-b572-4eff4fc046da" :answer/id #uuid "e928e1f5-d998-5632-b572-4eff4fc046da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.217" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106512 :question/problem "A softball team has a $70%$ chance of winning each game in a $10$-game season. What is the probability that the team wins $9$ of the $10$ games? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ We want to find the probability of winning $x=9$ games.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=9) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(1-0.7)^{10-9} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{9!(10-9)!}(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1} \\\\  & =10(0.7)^{9}(0.3) \\\\  & =0.121\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3887d38e-9181-547d-8de9-9f160529041d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3887d38e-9181-547d-8de9-9f160529041d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3887d38e-9181-547d-8de9-9f160529041d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-692b3019-71a9-5b63-9e3c-abdf054e7e2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db8a2a09-b8f1-5b4e-9e51-08645ed163c6" "answer-692b3019-71a9-5b63-9e3c-abdf054e7e2d" "answer-855beeb5-4f39-591a-a8ed-3abb6b72c857" "answer-64357ced-be65-5f92-896c-49a39f578379" "answer-ceb328de-2e12-51ae-a30e-e7eea7510405"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db8a2a09-b8f1-5b4e-9e51-08645ed163c6" :answer/id #uuid "db8a2a09-b8f1-5b4e-9e51-08645ed163c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.092" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-692b3019-71a9-5b63-9e3c-abdf054e7e2d" :answer/id #uuid "692b3019-71a9-5b63-9e3c-abdf054e7e2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.121" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-855beeb5-4f39-591a-a8ed-3abb6b72c857" :answer/id #uuid "855beeb5-4f39-591a-a8ed-3abb6b72c857" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.159" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64357ced-be65-5f92-896c-49a39f578379" :answer/id #uuid "64357ced-be65-5f92-896c-49a39f578379" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.257" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ceb328de-2e12-51ae-a30e-e7eea7510405" :answer/id #uuid "ceb328de-2e12-51ae-a30e-e7eea7510405" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.688" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-48a19d94-de64-5ea0-b742-40601b9c76a4" :question/id #uuid "48a19d94-de64-5ea0-b742-40601b9c76a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-331491" :question/problem "A bag contains $5$ green marbles, $7$ yellow marbles, and $8$ black marbles. An experiment of randomly selecting one marble from the bag and putting it back is repeated $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a black marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a black marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ selections.\n\n- The probability of selecting a black marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{8}{5+7+8}=\\frac{8}{20}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{2}{5}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{3}){(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{6-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{3!(6-3)!}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =20{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈0.276,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1404ccfc-3152-5913-b96d-4cc109f39c7d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1404ccfc-3152-5913-b96d-4cc109f39c7d" :answer-field/id #uuid "1404ccfc-3152-5913-b96d-4cc109f39c7d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a" :answer/id #uuid "181dd9a2-8771-56c4-9c66-782e9a17198a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.276" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c93c3864-ab3f-5794-a142-3eab6e62d56b" :question/id #uuid "c93c3864-ab3f-5794-a142-3eab6e62d56b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331492" :question/problem "A bag contains $9$ blue marbles, $5$ red marbles, and $6$ green marbles. A marble is drawn at random and replaced before the next draw. This process is repeated $7$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of selecting a blue marble exactly $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a blue marble is selected.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ trials.\n\n- The probability of selecting a blue marble is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{9+5+6}=\\frac{9}{20}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,\\frac{9}{20}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=3$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3}){(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{9}{20})}^{7-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{3!(7-3)!}{(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(1-\\frac{9}{20})}^{7-3} \\\\  & =35{(\\frac{9}{20})}^{3}{(\\frac{11}{20})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.292,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c72832e-c3d2-5add-a583-b41863a1a684"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c72832e-c3d2-5add-a583-b41863a1a684" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72832e-c3d2-5add-a583-b41863a1a684" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4da32402-ac89-5a1b-a640-f9d556346571" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4da32402-ac89-5a1b-a640-f9d556346571"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4da32402-ac89-5a1b-a640-f9d556346571" :answer/id #uuid "4da32402-ac89-5a1b-a640-f9d556346571" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.292" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-265e293f-7554-5958-abfd-df783c671c82" :question/id #uuid "265e293f-7554-5958-abfd-df783c671c82" :question/math-academy-id "q-89399" :question/problem "It is known that $41%$ of a given population have blood type O+. What is the probability that at most $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type O+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people with blood type O+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ trials\n\n- the probability of finding a person with blood type O+ is\n\n$$\np=0.41\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.41).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(1-0.41)}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(0.41)}^{x}{(0.59)}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting a \"success\" (i.e., a person with blood type O+) on at most $3$ trials. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0}){(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1}){(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2}){(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3}){(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & ={(0.41)}^{0}{(0.59)}^{10}+10{(0.41)}^{1}{(0.59)}^{9}+45{(0.41)}^{2}{(0.59)}^{8}+120{(0.41)}^{3}{(0.59)}^{7} \\\\  & =0.358\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c3d9610-1715-554d-8262-9b8d134a3c3f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c3d9610-1715-554d-8262-9b8d134a3c3f" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3d9610-1715-554d-8262-9b8d134a3c3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ea6bbf8-7531-55c5-bbc3-cab16fbb769f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c6121925-4323-53df-85ac-9e96651a8679" "answer-247eb43d-feb9-5cee-9894-cb5032551bdc" "answer-af3fd1a3-b502-54c5-8842-d31fc8bf7413" "answer-3ea6bbf8-7531-55c5-bbc3-cab16fbb769f" "answer-a2efb8b4-7301-5268-9953-cc95879d588d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6121925-4323-53df-85ac-9e96651a8679" :answer/id #uuid "c6121925-4323-53df-85ac-9e96651a8679" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.152" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-247eb43d-feb9-5cee-9894-cb5032551bdc" :answer/id #uuid "247eb43d-feb9-5cee-9894-cb5032551bdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.318" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af3fd1a3-b502-54c5-8842-d31fc8bf7413" :answer/id #uuid "af3fd1a3-b502-54c5-8842-d31fc8bf7413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.321" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ea6bbf8-7531-55c5-bbc3-cab16fbb769f" :answer/id #uuid "3ea6bbf8-7531-55c5-bbc3-cab16fbb769f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.358" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2efb8b4-7301-5268-9953-cc95879d588d" :answer/id #uuid "a2efb8b4-7301-5268-9953-cc95879d588d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.385" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec42c1b6-69ea-5d9d-b8b5-5ad8c82e5789" :question/id #uuid "ec42c1b6-69ea-5d9d-b8b5-5ad8c82e5789" :question/math-academy-id "q-331525" :question/problem "A basketball player has a $50%$ chance of making a free throw. If they take $7$ shots, what is the probability that they make at most $3$ of them?\n\nThe probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=7$ shots.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=50%=0.5.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,0.5).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(1-0.5)^{7-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{x}(0.5)^{7-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that $3$ or fewer of the $7$ shots are successful. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & ={\\sum }_{x=0}^{3}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{x})(0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{0})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{1})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{2})+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})](0.5)^{7} \\\\  & =(1+7+21+35)\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1}{128} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6dd545c-98d8-56db-ace2-b43058497897"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6dd545c-98d8-56db-ace2-b43058497897" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6dd545c-98d8-56db-ace2-b43058497897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7" :answer/id #uuid "18ce8a43-15dd-5064-88bf-0a2b654ec3d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0df1382-8e7a-5174-b609-3e041c2744d5" :question/id #uuid "f0df1382-8e7a-5174-b609-3e041c2744d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-84797" :question/problem "On a particular webpage, there is an $80%$ probability that each downloaded file does not contain a computer virus. If $6$ files are downloaded, what is the probability that at least $5$ of the files are virus-free? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of virus-free files.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ files\n\n- the probability that each file is virus-free is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least $5$ of the $6$ files are virus-free. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =P(X=5)+P(X=6) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.8)^{5}(0.2)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.8)^{6}(0.2)^{6-6} \\\\  & =6(0.8)^{5}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{6} \\\\  & =0.655\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b50d0c55-e310-5f1f-ba20-584d4a9a1df5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b50d0c55-e310-5f1f-ba20-584d4a9a1df5" :answer-field/id #uuid "b50d0c55-e310-5f1f-ba20-584d4a9a1df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8312f94-a2cf-5afb-8d33-ce7a63d7d6bc" "answer-22ff577d-7f26-5b83-b643-9fce2c24c06e" "answer-26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" "answer-7cddf7d6-cafe-5fe4-969c-5fecde39057e" "answer-dad7de5f-fb75-59c7-891d-0b4f384f052d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8312f94-a2cf-5afb-8d33-ce7a63d7d6bc" :answer/id #uuid "d8312f94-a2cf-5afb-8d33-ce7a63d7d6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.344" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22ff577d-7f26-5b83-b643-9fce2c24c06e" :answer/id #uuid "22ff577d-7f26-5b83-b643-9fce2c24c06e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.618" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" :answer/id #uuid "26bdf208-87cb-509f-96c8-8875887e81ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.655" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cddf7d6-cafe-5fe4-969c-5fecde39057e" :answer/id #uuid "7cddf7d6-cafe-5fe4-969c-5fecde39057e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.715" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dad7de5f-fb75-59c7-891d-0b4f384f052d" :answer/id #uuid "dad7de5f-fb75-59c7-891d-0b4f384f052d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.737" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-90500394-6fc0-5ba4-a5dc-ea52dd5425de" :question/id #uuid "90500394-6fc0-5ba4-a5dc-ea52dd5425de" :question/math-academy-id "q-89398" :question/problem "A basketball player practices shooting $10$ free throws. The player has an $80%$ chance of making a basket in each free throw. What is the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of baskets that the player makes.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ free throws\n\n- the probability of making a basket in each free throw is $p=80%=0.8$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.8).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(1-0.8)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.8)^{x}(0.2)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the basketball player makes more than $8$ baskets. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>8) & =P(X=9)+P(X=10) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.8)^{9}(0.2)^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.8)^{10}(0.2)^{0} \\\\  & =10(0.8)^{9}(0.2)+1(0.8)^{10} \\\\  & =0.376\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc6dbfdb-46b1-5bb4-ba87-2a3e95b166df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc6dbfdb-46b1-5bb4-ba87-2a3e95b166df" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc6dbfdb-46b1-5bb4-ba87-2a3e95b166df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2cbaf130-f38c-5cec-b719-9fe23cda6746" :answer-field/choices ["answer-24186a4f-baf5-5bbf-8437-a6d633e7ff67" "answer-b09d1f6a-90b7-5eb8-a6f9-4d7a285a4280" "answer-2cbaf130-f38c-5cec-b719-9fe23cda6746" "answer-13359ee6-0e60-5eae-8685-260358b6d02a" "answer-fec98c8f-bd15-5730-a9c2-11f482fcd350"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24186a4f-baf5-5bbf-8437-a6d633e7ff67" :answer/id #uuid "24186a4f-baf5-5bbf-8437-a6d633e7ff67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.317" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b09d1f6a-90b7-5eb8-a6f9-4d7a285a4280" :answer/id #uuid "b09d1f6a-90b7-5eb8-a6f9-4d7a285a4280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.345" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cbaf130-f38c-5cec-b719-9fe23cda6746" :answer/id #uuid "2cbaf130-f38c-5cec-b719-9fe23cda6746" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.376" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13359ee6-0e60-5eae-8685-260358b6d02a" :answer/id #uuid "13359ee6-0e60-5eae-8685-260358b6d02a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.397" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fec98c8f-bd15-5730-a9c2-11f482fcd350" :answer/id #uuid "fec98c8f-bd15-5730-a9c2-11f482fcd350" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.406" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-69ccd3d4-6bc6-5b97-931d-42636fcb0b40" :question/id #uuid "69ccd3d4-6bc6-5b97-931d-42636fcb0b40" :question/math-academy-id "q-130748" :question/problem "$70%$ of union members are in favor of a specific change to their conditions of employment. A random sample of $6$ members is taken. What is the probability that at most $2$ members are in favor of the changed conditions? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of members in the sample in favor of the change.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ members\n\n- since $70%$ of union members are in favor of the change, the probability of selecting a member in favor of the change is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(1-0.7)}^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){(0.7)}^{x}{(0.3)}^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most two members are in favor of the changed conditions. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0}){(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1}){(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{6-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{6-2} \\\\  & =1{(0.7)}^{0}{(0.3)}^{6}+6{(0.7)}^{1}{(0.3)}^{5}+15{(0.7)}^{2}{(0.3)}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.070\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e65c8161-0653-52f7-980c-c7f61d6aeb33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e65c8161-0653-52f7-980c-c7f61d6aeb33" :answer-field/id #uuid "e65c8161-0653-52f7-980c-c7f61d6aeb33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e1db3c8b-0f51-5da7-b9c0-02c9c8fda687" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e3ef3da-4f6a-57f3-b072-97ce1d7304c1" "answer-e1db3c8b-0f51-5da7-b9c0-02c9c8fda687" "answer-177c5f24-3832-5fee-961f-92a28b65ff5f" "answer-d4345274-9a8a-5da8-9701-5446ba840e90" "answer-30a08fd6-9eca-5ed7-96b9-1e94fa413565"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e3ef3da-4f6a-57f3-b072-97ce1d7304c1" :answer/id #uuid "8e3ef3da-4f6a-57f3-b072-97ce1d7304c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.059" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1db3c8b-0f51-5da7-b9c0-02c9c8fda687" :answer/id #uuid "e1db3c8b-0f51-5da7-b9c0-02c9c8fda687" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.070" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-177c5f24-3832-5fee-961f-92a28b65ff5f" :answer/id #uuid "177c5f24-3832-5fee-961f-92a28b65ff5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.173" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4345274-9a8a-5da8-9701-5446ba840e90" :answer/id #uuid "d4345274-9a8a-5da8-9701-5446ba840e90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.272" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-30a08fd6-9eca-5ed7-96b9-1e94fa413565" :answer/id #uuid "30a08fd6-9eca-5ed7-96b9-1e94fa413565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.989" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a3cf3a8-b182-51ac-8e52-79ae8f41f3ac" :question/id #uuid "7a3cf3a8-b182-51ac-8e52-79ae8f41f3ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-84807" :question/problem "In a bag containing candies, there is a $70%$ chance of getting a red candy when a random candy is taken. If $16$ candies are removed from the bag, what is the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\nYou may assume that the bag contains a large number of candies and that the probability of selecting a red candy is the same on each trial." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of red candies.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=16$ candies\n\n- the probability of getting a red candy is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(16,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{16-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{16-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that fewer than $15$ candies are red. This corresponds to $P(X<15).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X<15)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<15) & =1-P(X\\ge 15) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=15)+P(X=16)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{15})(0.7)^{15}(0.3)^{16-15}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{16}{16})(0.7)^{16}(0.3)^{16-16}] \\\\  & =1-[16(0.7)^{15}(0.3)+(0.7)^{16}] \\\\  & =1-0.026\\,112 \\\\  & =0.974\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a3e2700b-68a3-5d33-878c-99b41a013f77"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a3e2700b-68a3-5d33-878c-99b41a013f77" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e2700b-68a3-5d33-878c-99b41a013f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3ca9df2e-63f3-5b2f-8b41-7f7f2f9a6582" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5d36912d-c8ee-5759-a4ba-72a04e64cba0" "answer-ad502918-2f4f-5ee1-ab53-423479807ff6" "answer-ec808eb3-43a1-5e60-8d02-f99e9dec48f5" "answer-3ca9df2e-63f3-5b2f-8b41-7f7f2f9a6582" "answer-eb6149b3-e75f-58c2-b376-432afa2a115b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d36912d-c8ee-5759-a4ba-72a04e64cba0" :answer/id #uuid "5d36912d-c8ee-5759-a4ba-72a04e64cba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.026" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad502918-2f4f-5ee1-ab53-423479807ff6" :answer/id #uuid "ad502918-2f4f-5ee1-ab53-423479807ff6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.871" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec808eb3-43a1-5e60-8d02-f99e9dec48f5" :answer/id #uuid "ec808eb3-43a1-5e60-8d02-f99e9dec48f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.897" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ca9df2e-63f3-5b2f-8b41-7f7f2f9a6582" :answer/id #uuid "3ca9df2e-63f3-5b2f-8b41-7f7f2f9a6582" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.974" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb6149b3-e75f-58c2-b376-432afa2a115b" :answer/id #uuid "eb6149b3-e75f-58c2-b376-432afa2a115b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.996" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cfd01f57-c0cd-576d-bb6c-c4a4c9533c2d" :question/id #uuid "cfd01f57-c0cd-576d-bb6c-c4a4c9533c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-131048" :question/problem "A box of chocolates contains cherry creams and almond little cakes in the ratio of $3:2.$ Ten chocolates are selected at random, with replacement. What is the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of selected cherry creams.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ selected chocolates\n\n- since cherry creams and almond little cakes are in the ratio of $3:2,$ the probability of selecting a cherry cream from the box is $p=\\frac{3}{5}$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,\\frac{3}{5}).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{3}{5})}^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting at most $9$ cherry creams. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 9).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 9)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=9)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 9) & =1-P(X>9) \\\\  & =1-P(X=10) \\\\  & =1-(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10}){(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{10-10} \\\\  & =1-1{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{10}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0} \\\\  & =1-0.006\\,047 \\\\  & ≈0.994\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d275997-d198-54d6-a316-0c412fd339be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d275997-d198-54d6-a316-0c412fd339be" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d275997-d198-54d6-a316-0c412fd339be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3383f195-912b-5799-ad90-e9e9dc9bee9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf599ee1-76c6-5863-8598-76bbd4c27c9d" "answer-f46341a5-5f4f-578d-831f-6f6149790cb2" "answer-33f90eab-98a8-56e1-ba68-6b050ad4df9b" "answer-72382a44-6c7d-56b0-818d-1f68163d43f1" "answer-3383f195-912b-5799-ad90-e9e9dc9bee9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf599ee1-76c6-5863-8598-76bbd4c27c9d" :answer/id #uuid "bf599ee1-76c6-5863-8598-76bbd4c27c9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.040" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f46341a5-5f4f-578d-831f-6f6149790cb2" :answer/id #uuid "f46341a5-5f4f-578d-831f-6f6149790cb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.135" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33f90eab-98a8-56e1-ba68-6b050ad4df9b" :answer/id #uuid "33f90eab-98a8-56e1-ba68-6b050ad4df9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.846" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72382a44-6c7d-56b0-818d-1f68163d43f1" :answer/id #uuid "72382a44-6c7d-56b0-818d-1f68163d43f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.927" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3383f195-912b-5799-ad90-e9e9dc9bee9c" :answer/id #uuid "3383f195-912b-5799-ad90-e9e9dc9bee9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.994" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6d707ab1-b369-599a-9705-58f642e1bb10" :question/id #uuid "6d707ab1-b369-599a-9705-58f642e1bb10" :question/math-academy-id "q-130880" :question/problem "An infectious flu virus is spreading through a school. The probability of a randomly selected student having the flu next week is $0.3.$ If a class has $25$ students, what is the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of students having the flu next week.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=25$ students in the class\n\n- the probability of selecting a student having the flu next week is $0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(25,0.3).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(1-0.3)^{25-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{25-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that two or more students from the class will have the flu next week. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=25)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{25}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{25-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{25}+25(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{24}] \\\\  & =1-0.001\\,571 \\\\  & ≈0.998\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e3ae2aad-9d53-58ac-93a0-f36b60a4bfa8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e3ae2aad-9d53-58ac-93a0-f36b60a4bfa8" :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ae2aad-9d53-58ac-93a0-f36b60a4bfa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62e8c490-2c01-565b-af6a-a26106979fc0" "answer-0dfd0d54-03a3-56e8-826f-a80849a9aeef" "answer-38fd8a19-3d7c-53ef-8685-0b3fe7865ee9" "answer-f73ed5db-8aa0-5ab5-9fa9-5db0867880fa" "answer-097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62e8c490-2c01-565b-af6a-a26106979fc0" :answer/id #uuid "62e8c490-2c01-565b-af6a-a26106979fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.007" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dfd0d54-03a3-56e8-826f-a80849a9aeef" :answer/id #uuid "0dfd0d54-03a3-56e8-826f-a80849a9aeef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.009" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38fd8a19-3d7c-53ef-8685-0b3fe7865ee9" :answer/id #uuid "38fd8a19-3d7c-53ef-8685-0b3fe7865ee9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.889" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f73ed5db-8aa0-5ab5-9fa9-5db0867880fa" :answer/id #uuid "f73ed5db-8aa0-5ab5-9fa9-5db0867880fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.938" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07" :answer/id #uuid "097b49f8-fb5c-5334-83e4-04c6c6ba8e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.998" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1efc3f5-91c0-5d80-b7bf-74abaff81c0a" :question/id #uuid "b1efc3f5-91c0-5d80-b7bf-74abaff81c0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130882" :question/problem "$5%$ of electric bulbs are defective at manufacture. If six bulbs are randomly tested, with each one being replaced before the next is chosen, what is the probability that at least two are defective? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of defective tested bulbs. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=6$ tested bulbs\n\n- the probability of choosing a defective bulb is $p=5%=0.05$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.05).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(1-0.05)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.05)^{x}(0.95)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at least two bulbs are defective. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=6)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{6-1}] \\\\  & =1-[1(0.05)^{0}(0.95)^{6}+6(0.05)^{1}(0.95)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.967\\,226 \\\\  & ≈0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2c7a716-fc4c-5004-8bf8-cfd3d0945b59"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2c7a716-fc4c-5004-8bf8-cfd3d0945b59" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2c7a716-fc4c-5004-8bf8-cfd3d0945b59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" "answer-16e1f5c3-3735-584e-8765-6dea06b02e2e" "answer-6412fbb1-01e9-5e4f-95c5-7d8cf906c60a" "answer-eb8368ff-4749-5812-ba39-ab317a2cc469" "answer-6d1a31e0-ee07-527f-ba69-44baa27e2377"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" :answer/id #uuid "0f3e2937-9ade-57cc-a4fa-b599e5da4a9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.033" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16e1f5c3-3735-584e-8765-6dea06b02e2e" :answer/id #uuid "16e1f5c3-3735-584e-8765-6dea06b02e2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.257" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6412fbb1-01e9-5e4f-95c5-7d8cf906c60a" :answer/id #uuid "6412fbb1-01e9-5e4f-95c5-7d8cf906c60a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.333" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb8368ff-4749-5812-ba39-ab317a2cc469" :answer/id #uuid "eb8368ff-4749-5812-ba39-ab317a2cc469" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.891" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d1a31e0-ee07-527f-ba69-44baa27e2377" :answer/id #uuid "6d1a31e0-ee07-527f-ba69-44baa27e2377" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.967" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a48cf853-bbc5-5226-99de-0617e7d6b622" :question/id #uuid "a48cf853-bbc5-5226-99de-0617e7d6b622" :question/math-academy-id "q-331962" :question/problem "A drone has a $65%$ chance of successfully delivering a package. It makes $5$ deliveries in one day. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that it succeeds at most $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful deliveries.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ deliveries.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=65%=0.65.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.65).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(1-0.65)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.65)^{x}(0.35)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the drone succeeds at most $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.65)^{4}(0.35)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.65)^{5}(0.35)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.65)^{4}(0.35)+(0.65)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.428\\,415 \\\\  & ≈0.572,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82a882d2-167d-5383-9684-88e207eea076"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82a882d2-167d-5383-9684-88e207eea076" :answer-field/id #uuid "82a882d2-167d-5383-9684-88e207eea076" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6b2e490a-1bb1-5990-9e77-13fbe35019de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b2e490a-1bb1-5990-9e77-13fbe35019de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b2e490a-1bb1-5990-9e77-13fbe35019de" :answer/id #uuid "6b2e490a-1bb1-5990-9e77-13fbe35019de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.572" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107105 :tutorial/content "A **binomial distribution** is a probability distribution that models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (or trials) where each outcome is either success or failure. In a binomial distribution, the probability of success in each experiment is the same.\n\nIf there are $n$ experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of successes has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\binom{n}{x} p^x (1-p)^{n-x}, \\qquad x=0,1,2, \\ldots, n\n$$\n\nFor example, if we flip a coin $5$ times, then the number of heads follows a binomial distribution.\n\n- Each toss is an experiment that either results in success (i.e., heads) or failure (i.e., tails).\n\n- There are $n=5$ experiments.\n\n- Each experiment has a probability $p=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of success.\n\nSo, the number of heads has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x}){(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen a random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution consisting of $n$ experiments (or trials) and the probability of success on each trial is $p,$ we often write\n\n$$\nX\\sim B(n,p).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn words, we say, \"the random variable $X$ follows a binomial distribution with $n$ trials and probability $p$ of success on each trial.\"" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106014 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
